{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added a per-user feed limit (`max_feeds`, 0 = unlimited). The build and existing unit and integration tests pass; the new behaviour itself is not covered by any test I could run.\n\n- **Schema and model:** a new migration adds `users.max_feeds` (int, default 0). The field is returned on the user object by every user lookup.\n- **Enforcement:** the limit is checked inside `Storage.CreateFeed`, the only place that inserts feeds. That covers the API, OPML import, the web UI and the Google Reader API. The check and the insert share one transaction that locks the user row, so concurrent requests cannot exceed the limit.\n- **`POST /v1/feeds`:** returns 400 and creates nothing once the user is at the limit.\n- **`POST /v1/import`:** imports feeds until the limit is reached, then stops and returns 400. It is a partial import, not all-or-nothing.\n- **`PUT /v1/users/{userID}`:** an administrator can set `max_feeds`; a negative value returns 400. A non-admin who sends a different value gets 403; sending the current value unchanged is accepted.\n\nThings you might not expect:\n- `max_feeds` is written by a separate `SetUserMaxFeeds` call rather than by `UpdateUser`, so a user saving their own settings can never overwrite it. The trade-off is that the admin update is two writes, not one atomic one.\n- The limit error message is a plain English string, not a translation key; I did not add entries to the locale files.\n- Lowering `max_feeds` below a user's current feed count deletes nothing; it only blocks new feeds.\n- There is no web UI control for the limit; it is settable through the API only.", "t": 1794462836.058}
{"tool": "_result", "name": "finish", "chars": 55, "t": 1794462836.059}
